    "q-143197": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143197",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ is a sample of size $n=100$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9$ and the sample size $n=100,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{9}{100} \\\\  & =0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
