    "q-143424": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143424",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is a sample of size $n=8$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=16$ and the sample size is $n=8,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{2}≈1.41.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
